            <ArticleTitle>Endothelial dysfunction in systemic lupus erythematosus: evaluation with 13N-ammonia PET.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1927-31</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2967/jnumed.110.078212</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="UNLABELLED">Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) affects multiple organs and systems, severely involving the cardiovascular system. The aim of this study was to evaluate the presence of endothelial dysfunction with (13)N-ammonia PET in asymptomatic SLE patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We enrolled 16 women with SLE and 16 healthy women. Myocardial blood flow (MBF) was quantified in a 64-slice PET/CT scanner at rest, during a cold pressor test (CPT), and during stress. Endothelium-dependent vasodilation index, %ΔMBF, and myocardial flow reserve (MFR) were calculated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There were 16 women in the SLE group (mean age ± SD, 31.4 ± 8.3 y) and 16 women in the healthy control group (31.5 ± 11.1 y). Mean endothelium-dependent vasodilatation index and %ΔMBF were significantly lower in SLE patients (1.18 ± 0.55 vs. 1.63 ± 0.65, P = 0.04, and 18 ± 55 vs. 63 ± 65, P = 0.04, respectively). MFR was also lower in the SLE group (2.41 ± 0.59 vs. 2.73 ± 0.77, P = 0.20).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">SLE patients who are free of active disease present abnormal coronary flow and endothelial dysfunction. It is necessary to develop and intensify treatment strategies directed to CAD in SLE patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
